 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** The computational cost per step is similar to the exponential model, involving a few floating-point operations (power, division, multiplication). The inverse CDF sampling requires a power operation, which is slightly more expensive than the logarithm used in exponential sampling, but still constant time $O(1)$ per step [G].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ per particle/step. No additional data structures are required [G].
-- **Strengths:** Provides artistic control over light bleeding depth. The long-tail distribution allows a small fraction of photons to travel much deeper than an exponential distribution would allow, which can be useful for simulating subsurface scattering in materials like skin [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** The model assumes a homogeneous medium with positively correlated particle distribution. The parameter $a$ (or $b$) requires tuning to achieve desired visual results [G].
